(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["window",{"l:window/fixed-size-windows-one-in-one-out":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Adjacent windows of size k share k - 1 elements, so never recompute: add the element that enters, remove the one that leaves, and read the answer. Any aggregate with an inverse works this way (sum, count, letter counts, XOR). The first window costs O(k) and every slide O(1): O(n) in all instead of O(nk).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Invariant\u003c/b>: after processing index \u003ccode>r\u003c/code> (with \u003ccode>r &gt;= k - 1\u003c/code>), the state describes exactly \u003ccode>a[r - k + 1 .. r]\u003c/code>. Each step: add \u003ccode>a[r]\u003c/code>; if \u003ccode>r &gt;= k\u003c/code>, remove \u003ccode>a[r - k]\u003c/code>; then read.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Aggregates without an inverse\u003c/b>, such as maximum and minimum, cannot be \"un-added\". Pair the window with a monotonic deque (O(1) amortised, a later card) or a sorted container (O(log k)).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Matching a multiset\u003c/b> (is some window a rearrangement of \u003ccode>p\u003c/code>?). Comparing two \u003ccode>Counter\u003c/code>s per slide costs O(alphabet) and allocates. Instead keep \u003ccode>diff[c] = window count - p's count\u003c/code> for each letter and a number \u003ccode>bad\u003c/code> of letters with \u003ccode>diff != 0\u003c/code>. A slide changes two letters, so \u003ccode>bad\u003c/code> changes in O(1), and a match is \u003ccode>bad == 0\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Python specifics.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>sum(a[i:i + k])\u003c/code> inside a loop copies k references every time: O(nk). For a stream you cannot index back into, \u003ccode>collections.deque(maxlen=k)\u003c/code> keeps the last k items and drops the oldest automatically in O(1). Floating-point sums drift after millions of add-and-subtract steps; use integers (cents, milliseconds) or recompute the sum periodically.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Averages\u003c/b>: for a fixed k, maximise the sum and divide once at the end.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def window_sums(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Sum of every length-k window, left to right (empty if k &gt; len(a)).\"\"\"\n    if k &gt; len(a):\n        return []\n    s = sum(a[:k])\n    out = [s]\n    for r in range(k, len(a)):\n        s += a[r] - a[r - k]          # a[r] enters, a[r - k] leaves\n        out.append(s)\n    return out\n\n\ndef same_letter_windows(s, p):\n    \"\"\"Starts of the len(p)-long windows of s with exactly p's letter counts (letters a-z).\"\"\"\n    k = len(p)\n    if k == 0 or k &gt; len(s):\n        return []\n    diff = [0] * 26                   # window count minus p's count, per letter\n    for c in p:\n        diff[ord(c) - 97] -= 1\n    bad = sum(1 for d in diff if d)   # letters whose counts still differ\n    out = []\n\n    def bump(c, delta):\n        nonlocal bad\n        i = ord(c) - 97\n        bad -= diff[i] != 0\n        diff[i] += delta\n        bad += diff[i] != 0\n\n    for r, c in enumerate(s):\n        bump(c, +1)\n        if r &gt;= k:\n            bump(s[r - k], -1)\n        if r &gt;= k - 1 and bad == 0:\n            out.append(r - k + 1)\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Window sums of \u003ccode>[4, 2, 7, 1, 8, 3]\u003c/code> with k = 3; the highlighted cells are the current window.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First window a[0..2]: sum = 13, computed once in O(k)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index      0    1    2    3    4    5\na          \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    1    8    3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide: 1 enters, 4 leaves: sum = 10 (best 13). One addition and one subtraction, whatever k is\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          4    \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>    8    3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide: 8 enters, 2 leaves: sum = 16 (best 16). One addition and one subtraction, whatever k is\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          4    2    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide: 3 enters, 7 leaves: sum = 12 (best 16). One addition and one subtraction, whatever k is\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          4    2    7    \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Off-by-one: the first full window ends at \u003ccode>r = k - 1\u003c/code>; record only from there. If \u003ccode>k &gt; n\u003c/code> there is no window at all.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>ord(c) - 97\u003c/code> assumes lowercase ASCII letters; for general text use a dict and the same \u003ccode>bad\u003c/code> counter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Rebuilding the window's \u003ccode>Counter\u003c/code> or slice on every step quietly turns O(n) into O(nk).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>same_letter_windows(\"cbabcacab\", \"abc\")\u003c/code> return?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>[0, 2, 3, 6]\u003c/code>: the windows \"cba\", \"abc\", \"bca\" and \"cab\" each contain one a, one b and one c; \"bab\", \"cac\" and \"aca\" do not.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to slide a fixed window in O(1) per step and match windows against a multiset with a single counter.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:window/variable-windows-grow-shrink-and-why-it-works":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Advance the right edge one step at a time and add the new element to the window's state. If the window became invalid, advance the left edge until it is valid again, then record. Both edges only move forward, so the scan is O(n) amortised. It is correct only when validity is \u003cb>monotone\u003c/b>: shrinking a valid window keeps it valid (longest problems), or growing a valid window keeps it valid (shortest problems).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Invariant (longest)\u003c/b>: after the shrink loop, \u003ccode>[left, right]\u003c/code> is the longest valid window that ends at \u003ccode>right\u003c/code>. For each right end the best start is the smallest valid one, and with monotone validity that start never moves backwards as \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> grows, so \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> never has to.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> moves n times and \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> moves at most n times \u003ci>in total\u003c/i>, so the inner \u003ccode>while\u003c/code> does O(n) work over the whole run, not per step: O(n) amortised, times the cost of one state update.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Shortest variant\u003c/b> (sum at least \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>, values non-negative): grow; while the window is valid, record its length and shrink. It needs the mirror condition: if \u003ccode>[l, r]\u003c/code> is valid, so is \u003ccode>[l, r + 1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Monotone validity, and the counterexample.\u003c/b> With negative numbers neither direction holds. Longest subarray with sum at most 0 in \u003ccode>[5, -6, 1]\u003c/code>: the window drops 5 (on its own it is invalid) and returns 2 for \u003ccode>[-6, 1]\u003c/code>, but the whole array sums to 0, length 3. A later negative value would have repaired the window that was thrown away. When values can be negative or the target is an exact sum, use prefix sums (with a hash map to count, or a sorted structure for inequalities): the next module.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>O(1) validity tests\u003c/b>: a running sum; a count map plus \"the key just added has count 2\"; \u003ccode>len(count)\u003c/code> distinct keys (delete zero counts); for character replacement, \u003ccode>length - max count &lt;= k\u003c/code>. That last one even works with \u003ccode>max count\u003c/code> kept as a running maximum that never decreases: a stale value can keep an invalid window alive, but only at a length some valid window already reached, so the recorded answer stays right.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Jumping instead of crawling\u003c/b>: for \"no repeats\", store each character's last index and set \u003ccode>left = max(left, last[c] + 1)\u003c/code>; same O(n), fewer steps.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def longest_no_repeat(s):\n    \"\"\"Length of the longest substring with no repeated character (grow, then shrink).\"\"\"\n    count = defaultdict(int)\n    left = best = 0\n    for right, c in enumerate(s):\n        count[c] += 1                          # grow: c enters\n        while count[c] &gt; 1:                    # invalid: shrink until valid again\n            count[s[left]] -= 1\n            left += 1\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)     # [left, right] is the longest valid window ending at right\n    return best\n\n\ndef shortest_sum_at_least(a, target):\n    \"\"\"Length of the shortest subarray with sum &gt;= target (a &gt;= 0), or 0 if none.\"\"\"\n    total = left = 0\n    best = inf\n    for right, x in enumerate(a):\n        total += x                             # grow\n        while total &gt;= target:                 # valid: record, then try a shorter one\n            best = min(best, right - left + 1)\n            total -= a[left]\n            left += 1\n    return 0 if best == inf else best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Longest substring of \u003ccode>\"abcbad\"\u003c/code> with no repeated character: the count map and a valid or invalid marker after each move.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow over 'a', 'b', 'c' (r = 0, 1, 2), adding each to the count map. No count above 1: \u003cb>valid\u003c/b>, best = 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   b   a   d\ncounts {a:1, b:1, c:1}   valid\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow: 'b' enters at r = 3. count['b'] = 2, so the window is \u003cb>invalid\u003c/b>\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   a   d\ncounts {a:1, b:2, c:1}   INVALID\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Shrink from the left until valid: drop 'a' (left = 1), still two b's; drop 'b' (left = 2). Valid again; best stays 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         a   b   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   a   d\ncounts {b:1, c:1}   valid\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow: 'a' enters at r = 4. No count above 1: \u003cb>valid\u003c/b>, length 3, best = 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         a   b   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   d\ncounts {a:1, b:1, c:1}   valid\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow: 'd' enters at r = 5. No count above 1: \u003cb>valid\u003c/b>, length 4, best = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         a   b   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>d\u003c/mark>\ncounts {a:1, b:1, c:1, d:1}   valid\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Recording inside the shrink loop for \"longest\" records invalid windows; recording after it for \"shortest\" misses the shorter valid ones.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Shrinking with \u003ccode>if\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>while\u003c/code>: one step may not restore validity.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With a \"sum at least target\" loop and \u003ccode>target &lt;= 0\u003c/code>, the loop can empty the window and push \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> past \u003ccode>right\u003c/code>; handle that case first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Applying the template to data with negative values. Before coding, check monotonicity with a brute force on small random inputs that include negatives.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>shortest_sum_at_least([1, 4, 2, 2, 5], 7)\u003c/code> return, and when is the answer first recorded?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>2. Lengths 3 are recorded at r = 2 ([1, 4, 2]), r = 3 ([4, 2, 2]) and r = 4 ([2, 2, 5]); at r = 4 the loop then drops the first 2, finds [2, 5] still sums to 7 and records 2.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know the grow-then-shrink template, why it is O(n) amortised, and how to check the monotone-validity condition that makes it correct.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:window/counting-windows-at-most-k-and-exactly-k":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>With monotone validity, the valid windows ending at \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> are exactly those starting anywhere in \u003ccode>[left, right]\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>right - left + 1\u003c/code> of them. Summing that over every right end counts all valid subarrays in O(n). \"Exactly K\" is not monotone, because shrinking can drop below K, but it is the difference of two monotone counts: exactly(K) = atMost(K) - atMost(K - 1).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why \u003ccode>right - left + 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/b> After the shrink loop \u003ccode>[left, right]\u003c/code> is valid, so every \u003ccode>[l, right]\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>l &gt;= left\u003c/code> is valid too (it is a shrunk version), and every \u003ccode>l &lt; left\u003c/code> is invalid (that is why \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> moved past it).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Distinct counts.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>len(count)\u003c/code> is the number of distinct values only if keys are deleted when their count reaches 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why \"exactly K\" resists one window.\u003c/b> When the window holds K distinct values, several starts can work for the same end (dropping a duplicate keeps K), and one left pointer cannot represent that range. Two monotone counts can.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\"At least\" conditions\u003c/b> stay true as the window grows. Either use the complement (for distinct values, atLeast(K) = n(n + 1)/2 - atMost(K - 1)), or shrink while the window still qualifies and then add \u003ccode>left\u003c/code>: every start before \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> qualified with this end.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Products\u003c/b> (positive values): keep the running product, shrink while it is at least \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, and return 0 at once when \u003ccode>k &lt;= 1\u003c/code>, since no product of positive integers is below 1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost\u003c/b>: two O(n) passes for exactly-K; the count map holds at most K + 1 keys.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_at_most(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Number of subarrays with at most k distinct values.\"\"\"\n    if k &lt; 0:\n        return 0\n    count = defaultdict(int)\n    left = total = 0\n    for right, x in enumerate(a):\n        count[x] += 1\n        while len(count) &gt; k:\n            y = a[left]\n            count[y] -= 1\n            if count[y] == 0:\n                del count[y]                   # keep len(count) == number of distinct values\n            left += 1\n        total += right - left + 1              # every start in [left, right] works with this end\n    return total\n\n\ndef count_exactly(a, k):\n    return count_at_most(a, k) - count_at_most(a, k - 1)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>count_at_most([1, 2, 1, 3, 3], 2)\u003c/code>: each step adds the number of valid subarrays that end at \u003ccode>r\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 0: 1 enters, 1 distinct (valid). Subarrays ending here with a valid start: 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4\na         \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   2   1   3   3\ncounts {1:1}   + 1   total 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 1: 2 enters, 2 distinct (valid). Subarrays ending here with a valid start: 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4\na         \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>   1   3   3\ncounts {1:1, 2:1}   + 2   total 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 2: 1 enters, 2 distinct (valid). Subarrays ending here with a valid start: 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4\na         \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   3   3\ncounts {1:2, 2:1}   + 3   total 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 3: 3 makes 3 distinct values, invalid. Shrink: drop 1 and 2 (left = 2). Now 2 subarrays end at r and start in [left, r]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4\na         1   2   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   3\ncounts {1:1, 3:1}   + 2   total 8\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 4: 3 enters, 2 distinct (valid). Subarrays ending here with a valid start: 3. atMost(2) = 11; atMost(1) = 6, so exactly 2 distinct: 11 - 6 = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4\na         1   2   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\ncounts {1:1, 3:2}   + 3   total 11\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keeping zero counts in the dict, so \u003ccode>len(count)\u003c/code> overstates the number of distinct values and the window shrinks too far.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Adding 1 per valid window instead of \u003ccode>right - left + 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>atMost(K - 1)\u003c/code> with K = 0: return 0 for a negative limit rather than running the loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In the product version without the \u003ccode>k &lt;= 1\u003c/code> check, the shrink loop runs \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> past \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> and divides by elements that are no longer in the window.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which subarrays make up \u003ccode>count_exactly([1, 2, 1, 3, 3], 2) = 5\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>[1, 2], [2, 1], [1, 2, 1], [1, 3] and [1, 3, 3]. atMost(2) = 11 counts these plus the six single-value subarrays ([1], [2], [1], [3], [3], [3, 3]), which atMost(1) = 6 removes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to count valid subarrays in O(n) with right - left + 1, and how to turn a non-monotone \"exactly\" into two monotone \"at most\" counts.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:window/minimum-covering-window-the-formed-counter":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Grow the right edge until the window covers every requirement, then shrink from the left while it still does, recording each covering window. To test \"covers\" in O(1), keep \u003ccode>formed\u003c/code>: the number of distinct required characters whose count in the window has reached its requirement. It changes only when a count crosses its threshold, upwards while growing and downwards while shrinking.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Monotone in the \"shortest\" direction\u003c/b>: if \u003ccode>[l, r]\u003c/code> covers \u003ccode>t\u003c/code>, every larger window does too. So this is the shortest-window template with a cheaper validity test.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Bookkeeping\u003c/b>: when \u003ccode>have[c]\u003c/code> becomes exactly \u003ccode>need[c]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>formed += 1\u003c/code> (a surplus copy does not count again). When a drop makes \u003ccode>have[d] &lt; need[d]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>formed -= 1\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>formed == required\u003c/code> exactly when the window covers \u003ccode>t\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost\u003c/b>: each edge moves at most m = len(s) times with O(1) work per move, plus O(n) to count \u003ccode>t\u003c/code>: O(m + n) time and O(alphabet) memory. Comparing two Counters at each step would cost O(alphabet) per step instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Record positions, not slices\u003c/b>: store \u003ccode>(length, start)\u003c/code> and slice once at the end; slicing on every improvement copies characters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Relatives\u003c/b>: the fixed-window \u003ccode>bad\u003c/code> counter for anagram windows is the same idea; \"substrings containing all three letters\" counts with the \"at least\" trick; windows measured in whole words step through the text one word length at a time.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def min_window(s, t):\n    \"\"\"Shortest substring of s containing every character of t with multiplicity, or ''.\"\"\"\n    need = Counter(t)\n    required = len(need)                       # distinct characters to satisfy\n    have = defaultdict(int)\n    formed = 0                                 # how many of them are satisfied now\n    best_len, best_start = inf, 0\n    left = 0\n    for right, c in enumerate(s):\n        have[c] += 1\n        if c in need and have[c] == need[c]:\n            formed += 1                        # c just became satisfied\n        while formed == required:\n            if right - left + 1 &lt; best_len:\n                best_len, best_start = right - left + 1, left\n            d = s[left]\n            have[d] -= 1\n            if d in need and have[d] &lt; need[d]:\n                formed -= 1                    # d just became unsatisfied\n            left += 1\n    return \"\" if best_len == inf else s[best_start:best_start + best_len]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>min_window(\"xcbcab\", \"abc\")\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>have\u003c/code> shows the required characters only, and the highlighted cells are the window.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow over x, c, b, c. The second c pushes have[c] to 2, more than needed; formed counts only characters whose need is met: c and b, so 2 of 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         \u003cmark>x\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   a   b\nhave {a:0, b:1, c:2}   formed 2/3   best -\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow: a arrives, have[a] reaches need[a], so formed = 3/3: the window covers t. Now shrink while it still does\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         \u003cmark>x\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   b\nhave {a:1, b:1, c:2}   formed 3/3   best -\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Record best = 'xcbca' (length 5). Drop 'x': not in t, formed stays 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         x   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   b\nhave {a:1, b:1, c:2}   formed 3/3   best xcbca\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Record best = 'cbca'. Drop 'c': have[c] falls 2 -&gt; 1, still &gt;= need, so formed stays 3 and shrinking continues\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         x   c   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   b\nhave {a:1, b:1, c:1}   formed 3/3   best cbca\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Record best = 'bca'. Drop 'b': have[b] falls below need, formed = 2. The window is no longer covering: stop shrinking, grow again\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         x   c   b   \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   b\nhave {a:1, b:0, c:1}   formed 2/3   best bca\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow: b arrives, formed = 3 again for 'cab' (length 3, not shorter than 'bca', so best stays). Drop 'c': formed = 2. The scan ends: the answer is 'bca'\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\ns         x   c   b   c   \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>\nhave {a:1, b:1, c:0}   formed 2/3   best bca\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Incrementing \u003ccode>formed\u003c/code> on every copy of a required character: with \u003ccode>t = \"aa\"\u003c/code>, a single \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> must not complete the requirement.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Testing \u003ccode>have[d] &lt;= need[d]\u003c/code> after the decrement instead of \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>formed\u003c/code> then drops while the window still covers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Using a \u003ccode>defaultdict\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>need\u003c/code> and reading \u003ccode>need[c]\u003c/code> for characters outside \u003ccode>t\u003c/code> inserts them, which breaks \u003ccode>len(need)\u003c/code> if it is computed later; test \u003ccode>c in need\u003c/code> first, as the template does.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty \u003ccode>t\u003c/code>, or a \u003ccode>t\u003c/code> longer than \u003ccode>s\u003c/code>: return \"\" without scanning.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>min_window(\"abxaab\", \"aab\")\u003c/code> return, and when does \u003ccode>formed\u003c/code> first reach 2?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\"aab\". formed reaches 2 at r = 3, when the window \"abxa\" first holds two a's and a b. Later windows of length 4 tie, and at r = 5 dropping the x leaves \"aab\", length 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to find minimum covering windows in O(m + n) with a satisfied-requirements counter.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:window/window-extremes-and-when-to-leave-the-window-beh":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A window needs an aggregate you can update when an element leaves and a validity rule that is monotone. Maximum and minimum cannot be \"un-added\", so pair the window with a \u003cb>monotonic deque\u003c/b> of indices (O(1) amortised) or a heap with lazy deletion (O(log n)). If validity is not monotone, the window is the wrong tool: switch to prefix sums, sort first, or fix one more parameter so that each pass becomes monotone.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deque invariant\u003c/b>: indices increase from front to back and their values strictly decrease, so the front is the maximum of the current window. A new value first pops every smaller or equal value from the back (they are older and smaller, so they can never be a maximum again), then the front is dropped if it has left the window. Each index is pushed once and popped at most once: O(n) total. The monotonic stacks module proves this in depth and reuses it for next-greater problems.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Lazy heap\u003c/b>: push \u003ccode>(-value, index)\u003c/code>; before reading, pop tops whose index has left the window. Stale entries can pile up inside the heap, so it may hold up to n items: O(n log n), simpler to adapt when the window moves irregularly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Python\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>collections.deque\u003c/code> appends and pops at both ends in O(1) (it is a linked list of 64-slot blocks); \u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code> shifts everything, O(n).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>When the plain window breaks, match the symptom:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Symptom\u003c/th>\u003cth>Repair\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Need the max or min of every window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>monotonic deque of indices\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Values can be negative, or the target is an exact sum\u003c/td>\u003ctd>prefix sums plus a hash map of earlier prefixes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Elements can be taken only from both ends (reduce x to zero)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>complement: the kept middle is one window with sum total - x\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The condition is about values, not positions (make k increments)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sort, then window over the sorted values\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Adding a character can help or hurt (every letter at least k times)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>fix the number of distinct letters u = 1..26; each pass is an at-most-u window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(26n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def window_max(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Maximum of every length-k window; deque holds indices with decreasing values.\"\"\"\n    dq, out = deque(), []\n    for r, x in enumerate(a):\n        while dq and a[dq[-1]] &lt;= x:\n            dq.pop()                           # smaller, older values can never be a maximum again\n        dq.append(r)\n        if dq[0] &lt;= r - k:\n            dq.popleft()                       # the front left the window\n        if r &gt;= k - 1:\n            out.append(a[dq[0]])\n    return out\n\n\ndef window_max_heap(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Same answers with a lazy-deletion heap: O(n log n).\"\"\"\n    heap, out = [], []\n    for r, x in enumerate(a):\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-x, r))\n        while heap[0][1] &lt;= r - k:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)                # stale: its index left the window\n        if r &gt;= k - 1:\n            out.append(-heap[0][0])\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maximum of every window of size 3 in \u003ccode>[5, 3, 4, 1, 2, 0]\u003c/code>; the deque line shows \u003ccode>index(value)\u003c/code> from front to back.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 0: 5 enters\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\na         \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   3   4   1   2   0\ndeque  0(5)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 1: 3 enters\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\na         \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   4   1   2   0\ndeque  0(5) 1(3)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 2: 4 enters; pop 3 from the back (smaller and older: never a maximum again); max = a[0] = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\na         \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   1   2   0\ndeque  0(5) 2(4)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 3: 1 enters; front index 0 left the window: popleft; max = a[2] = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\na         5   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   2   0\ndeque  2(4) 3(1)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 4: 2 enters; pop 1 from the back (smaller and older: never a maximum again); max = a[2] = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\na         5   3   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>   0\ndeque  2(4) 4(2)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">r = 5: 0 enters; front index 2 left the window: popleft; max = a[4] = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index     0   1   2   3   4   5\na         5   3   4   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\ndeque  4(2) 5(0)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Storing values instead of indices in the deque: you can no longer tell when the front has left the window.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Expiring the front with \u003ccode>if\u003c/code> works for fixed windows (at most one index leaves per step); a variable window whose left edge can jump needs \u003ccode>while\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Popping with \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> keeps equal values in the deque; still correct, just longer. Popping from the front when you meant the back breaks the invariant.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Forcing a window onto data with negative values: see the counterexample in the variable-window card.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which tool would you use for \"count subarrays whose sum is exactly k\" when values may be negative, and why not a window?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Prefix sums with a hash map of how often each earlier prefix sum occurred. With negative values, shrinking a window can raise or lower its sum, so there is no left edge that only moves forward; validity is not monotone.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>window_max([5, 3, 4, 1, 2, 0], 3)\u003c/code> return?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>[5, 4, 4, 2]\u003c/code>, as in the trace: 5 leaves at r = 3, 4 leaves at r = 5.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to track window maxima in O(n) and how to spot and repair the cases where a plain sliding window does not apply.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:window/rolling-metrics-over-a-log-stream":"\u003cp>Windows over time are what monitoring and rate limiting compute all day. Prometheus' \u003ccode>rate(x[5m])\u003c/code> aggregates each series over the trailing five minutes; a rate limiter asks \"how many requests has this client sent in the last minute?\" on every request; Flink and Kafka Streams run the same windows over event streams. Here you compute these metrics over a synthetic day of one million requests, check them against a naive scan, and measure the two shortcuts real limiters take: fixed clock-minute buckets and Cloudflare's two-counter estimate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>rolling_lab.py\u003c/code>. The time windows are half-open, \u003ccode>(t - width, t]\u003c/code>, and the log is sorted by time, so the left edge only moves forward.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\"\"\"The rolling-metrics lab from dsa/09-window.md, as printed there (run: python3 rolling_lab.py).\"\"\"\nimport random\nimport time\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\n\ndef make_log(n, seed=0):\n    \"\"\"n (timestamp, user) requests over one day, sorted by time, with a busy lunchtime and a few heavy users.\"\"\"\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    times = sorted(rng.uniform(0, 86_400) if rng.random() &lt; 0.8 else rng.gauss(45_000, 1_800) % 86_400\n                   for _ in range(n))\n    users = [rng.randrange(20) if rng.random() &lt; 0.3 else rng.randrange(100_000) for _ in range(n)]\n    return times, users\n\n\ndef last_minute_naive(times):\n    \"\"\"For each request i: how many requests fell in (t_i - 60, t_i]. Scans backwards: O(n * w).\"\"\"\n    out = []\n    for i, t in enumerate(times):\n        j = i\n        while j &gt;= 0 and times[j] &gt; t - 60:\n            j -= 1\n        out.append(i - j)\n    return out\n\n\ndef last_minute(times, width=60):\n    \"\"\"Same answers with a variable window: the left edge only moves forward. O(n).\"\"\"\n    out, left = [], 0\n    for right, t in enumerate(times):\n        while times[left] &lt;= t - width:\n            left += 1\n        out.append(right - left + 1)\n    return out\n\n\ndef distinct_users_naive(times, users, width=300):\n    out = []\n    for i, t in enumerate(times):\n        seen, j = set(), i\n        while j &gt;= 0 and times[j] &gt; t - width:\n            seen.add(users[j])\n            j -= 1\n        out.append(len(seen))\n    return out\n\n\ndef distinct_users(times, users, width=300):\n    \"\"\"Distinct users in (t - width, t] at each request: window plus a count map.\"\"\"\n    count = defaultdict(int)\n    out, left = [], 0\n    for right, t in enumerate(times):\n        count[users[right]] += 1\n        while times[left] &lt;= t - width:\n            u = users[left]\n            count[u] -= 1\n            if count[u] == 0:\n                del count[u]\n            left += 1\n        out.append(len(count))\n    return out\n\n\ndef fixed_minute_max(times):\n    \"\"\"Busiest clock minute ([60k, 60k + 60)): what a fixed-window counter sees.\"\"\"\n    buckets = defaultdict(int)\n    for t in times:\n        buckets[int(t // 60)] += 1\n    return max(buckets.values())\n\n\ndef sliding_counter_estimate(times, width=60):\n    \"\"\"Cloudflare-style estimate at each request: previous bucket weighted by overlap plus current bucket.\"\"\"\n    buckets = defaultdict(int)\n    out = []\n    for t in times:\n        b = int(t // width)\n        buckets[b] += 1\n        elapsed = t - b * width\n        out.append(buckets[b - 1] * (width - elapsed) / width + buckets[b])\n    return out\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    times, users = make_log(1_000_000)\n    small = times[:10_000]\n    t0 = time.perf_counter(); a = last_minute_naive(small); t1 = time.perf_counter()\n    b = last_minute(small); t2 = time.perf_counter()\n    assert a == b\n    print(f\"last minute, 10k requests: naive {t1 - t0:.2f} s, window {t2 - t1:.4f} s\")\n    t0 = time.perf_counter(); a = distinct_users_naive(small, users[:10_000]); t1 = time.perf_counter()\n    b = distinct_users(small, users[:10_000]); t2 = time.perf_counter()\n    assert a == b\n    print(f\"distinct users in 5 min, 10k requests: naive {t1 - t0:.2f} s, window {t2 - t1:.4f} s\")\n\n    t0 = time.perf_counter(); rate = last_minute(times); t1 = time.perf_counter()\n    dist = distinct_users(times, users); t2 = time.perf_counter()\n    print(f\"1M requests: last-minute counts {t1 - t0:.2f} s, distinct users {t2 - t1:.2f} s\")\n    busiest = max(range(len(times)), key=rate.__getitem__)\n    print(f\"busiest sliding minute: {rate[busiest]} requests, ending at t = {times[busiest]:.0f} s\")\n    print(f\"busiest clock minute (fixed window): {fixed_minute_max(times)} requests\")\n    print(f\"most distinct users in 5 min: {max(dist)}\")\n    est = sliding_counter_estimate(times)\n    err = [abs(e - r) / r for e, r in zip(est, rate)]\n    print(f\"sliding-counter estimate: mean relative error {sum(err) / len(err):.2%}, max {max(err):.1%}\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Before running it, predict: how much faster is the window than the naive scan for 10,000 requests, given that a minute holds a few hundred requests and five minutes a few thousand? Will the busiest clock minute be higher or lower than the busiest sliding minute?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>python3 rolling_lab.py\u003c/code> (about 20 seconds; the naive distinct-users scan is the slow part). The asserts prove the window versions agree with the naive ones.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Explain the fixed-window result: why can the busiest clock minute never exceed the busiest sliding minute, and why does a limit enforced on clock minutes let up to twice the limit through in 60 seconds (L requests at 0:59, L more at 1:00)?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Look at the two-counter estimate's error. Sort the requests by error and look at the timestamps of the worst ones, then compare the mean error during the lunchtime peak (40,000 &lt; t &lt; 50,000) with the rest of the day. Which is larger, and why?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Turn \u003ccode>last_minute\u003c/code> into a limiter: a class with \u003ccode>allow(t)\u003c/code> that keeps a \u003ccode>deque\u003c/code> of accepted timestamps for one client, pops from the left while the oldest is at least 60 seconds old, and accepts only if fewer than L remain. Test it with a burst of 2L requests around a minute boundary and check that it never accepts more than L in any 60-second span.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>For 10,000 requests the naive last-minute count takes about a second and the window a few milliseconds, a speed-up of over 100x; for distinct users the naive scan takes several seconds against tens of milliseconds. The gap grows with the number of requests per window, because the naive scan rereads the whole window for every request.\u003c/li>\u003cli>On a million requests both window passes finish in one or two seconds of pure Python.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With the seed in the script: busiest sliding minute 3,387 requests (during the lunchtime peak, around t = 44,800 s), busiest clock minute 3,288. A clock minute is one particular 60-second window, so it can only miss the peak, never exceed it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The two-counter estimate has a mean relative error of about 1% and a worst case of about 10%. The estimate assumes the previous minute's requests were spread evenly, and random clumps break that assumption; the worst cases sit off-peak, and the mean error is about 0.8% during the peak against 1.3% elsewhere, because relative noise shrinks as counts grow. Cloudflare measured something different, wrong allow or block decisions, and found them on 0.003% of 400 million requests: a limiter only needs the estimate to be accurate near the limit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The sliding-log limiter from step 6 is exact but stores one timestamp per accepted request per client; the two-counter version stores two integers. Cloudflare chose the estimate for this kind of reason: a per-request log was too costly in memory and processing, while two counters need only the GET, SET and INCR operations their memcached cluster offered atomically.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>","p:643":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a sequence of numbers and a length \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, find the greatest average of any contiguous block containing exactly \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> elements. For \u003ccode>[2, -1, 4, 3]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>k = 2\u003c/code>, the best block is \u003ccode>[4, 3]\u003c/code>, with average 3.5. The block cannot skip an element.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Assume \u003ccode>1 &lt;= k &lt;= len(nums)\u003c/code>, as in the task. Values can be negative, so a best sum must not be initialized to zero. Every candidate has the same positive denominator; comparing sums is equivalent to comparing averages.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The best average over contiguous blocks of exactly k elements.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two neighbouring blocks share all but two elements. What changes when the block moves by one?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Fixed-size window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep the block sum: add the element entering on the right and subtract the one leaving on the left. Track the best sum and divide by k once at the end.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each starting index, sum the next \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> entries and compare the resulting averages. There are \u003ccode>n-k+1\u003c/code> candidates, giving O((n-k+1)k) time and O(1) extra space. The work overlaps heavily because adjacent blocks share \u003ccode>k-1\u003c/code> entries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build the first sum once. To advance the block, subtract its outgoing element and add its incoming element. After the update, the maintained sum is exactly the sum of the new block. Induction over the starting index shows that all candidates are compared, and the maximum sum divided by \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> is the maximum average.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First block [2, -1] has sum 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best = 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Move right: remove 2, add 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sum = 3; best = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Move right: remove -1, add 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sum = 7; answer = 7 / 2 = \u003cmark>3.5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The sum is updated before comparison, so the final block is included. Only the running and best sums need to survive a step.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def max_average(nums, k):\n    window = sum(nums[:k])\n    best = window\n    for right in range(k, len(nums)):\n        window += nums[right] - nums[right - k]\n        best = max(best, window)\n    return best / k\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Time is O(n), including the initial sum; \u003ccode>nums[:k]\u003c/code> creates an O(k) temporary slice, which is the auxiliary space bound. A loop accumulating the first \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> elements would reduce extra space to O(1).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Initializing the best to zero fails for all-negative input.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> equals the sequence length, the first block is the only candidate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Remove index \u003ccode>right-k\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>right-k+1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the starting index, retaining the earliest index when sums tie.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compute all fixed-width sums to feed a stream of moving averages.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If lengths may differ, comparing sums is no longer sufficient to compare averages.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:209":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find the minimum length of a contiguous block of positive integers whose sum is at least a positive target. Return zero if no block qualifies. For target 7 and \u003ccode>[2, 3, 1, 2, 4, 3]\u003c/code>, the block \u003ccode>[4, 3]\u003c/code> gives length 2.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The target and all entries are strictly positive. A longer block cannot have a smaller sum, and removing an entry strictly decreases the sum. This monotonicity is the reason a one-way left pointer is safe. With negative entries the same argument breaks; for example, discarding a negative prefix can increase the sum.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The shortest contiguous run whose sum reaches a target, and every value is positive.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With positive values, what happens to the sum when you drop the leftmost element of a run that already reaches the target?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Variable sliding window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Extend right; while the sum is still at least the target, record the length and drop from the left. Positivity makes validity monotone, so the left edge never moves back and the scan is O(n).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try each start and extend to every end, maintaining a running sum. Compare every qualifying length. There are O(n²) start/end pairs, O(n²) time and O(1) extra space. Recomputing each sum from scratch would take even longer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Scan rightward while maintaining the sum of the inclusive window \u003ccode>[left, right]\u003c/code>. Once it reaches the target, record the length and remove the leftmost element. Repeat until the window is too small in sum. A discarded start has already been considered at the earliest right endpoint where it qualifies; any later endpoint would only make its block longer, so it cannot improve the answer.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Conversely, an optimal block cannot be skipped: when its right end arrives, either its start is still present and shrinking examines it, or that start was discarded earlier after producing an even shorter qualifying block. Each endpoint advances at most \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> times.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Target 7; add 2, 3, 1, 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sum = 8; record length 4; remove 2 =&gt; sum 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sum = 10; record length 4, remove 3; record length 3, remove 1\nsum = 6, so stop shrinking\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sum = 9; record length 3, remove 2; record length \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>, remove 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The remaining sum is 3, so there is no further qualifying window ending at the last element. The best observed length is 2.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def min_subarray_length(target, nums):\n    left = 0\n    total = 0\n    best = len(nums) + 1\n    for right, value in enumerate(nums):\n        total += value\n        while total &gt;= target:\n            best = min(best, right - left + 1)\n            total -= nums[left]\n            left += 1\n    return 0 if best == len(nums) + 1 else best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Both pointers move only forward, so total time is O(n), despite the nested loop. The running sum, pointers and best length use O(1) extra space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A singleton at least the target is already optimal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a \u003ccode>while\u003c/code>, not an \u003ccode>if\u003c/code>, to test every shorter qualifying window.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An unattainable target returns zero; empty input also returns zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>This implementation relies on a positive target and positive values.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return a pair of boundary indices together with the length.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For arbitrary signed integers, use prefix sums and a monotonic deque instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a fixed starting point, binary-search prefix sums when all values are positive.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:3":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a string, return the length of its longest contiguous substring in which every character is distinct. For \u003ccode>abba\u003c/code>, the answer is 2, achieved by either \u003ccode>ab\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>ba\u003c/code>. An empty string has answer zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Characters are compared as Python string characters; the method does not depend on a small alphabet. The desired object is contiguous, so deleting arbitrary repeated characters is not allowed. The dictionary may contain characters that no longer belong to the current window.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The longest substring with no character repeated.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Grow a run to the right. When a repeat appears, what is the least you must cut from the left?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Variable sliding window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep counts (or the last index) of each character in the window; on a repeat, move left past the earlier copy, never backwards (left = max(left, last[c] + 1)). Record the length after each repair.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(alphabet) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start at each position, extend until a character repeats, and maintain a set for that start. This tries O(n²) extensions in the worst case, with O(min(n, alphabet size)) set space. Rebuilding the set for every start repeats much of the same work.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep the last index of every character and a left boundary. Before processing the next character, the current window has no duplicates. If the character last appeared within the window, the only way to include the new occurrence without a repeat is to move left past the old one. If its previous occurrence is already outside, leave the boundary alone.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The updated window is the longest distinct suffix ending at this right index: every earlier start would contain both copies, while the retained suffix has no other repeats. Therefore taking the maximum over right endpoints considers an optimal answer. The boundary never moves backwards.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keeping old positions in the dictionary is safe: the boundary test distinguishes a repeat inside the active window from one that has already been excluded. This avoids deleting entries whenever the boundary advances and keeps the implementation simple.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read a then b\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window = ab, best = 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read second b at index 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>previous b at 1; left becomes 2; window = b\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read a at index 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>previous a at 0 is outside; left stays 2\nwindow = ba; best = \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The maximum with the existing left boundary is essential: the old \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> must not pull the window back across the repeated \u003ccode>b\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def longest_distinct(s):\n    last = {}\n    left = 0\n    best = 0\n    for right, char in enumerate(s):\n        if char in last:\n            left = max(left, last[char] + 1)\n        last[char] = right\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are O(n) expected dictionary operations and O(min(n, alphabet size)) auxiliary space for last positions. Hash table operation costs are expected bounds.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Setting \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> directly to an old index can move it backward on \u003ccode>abba\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Update the last position after using the prior one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Spaces, punctuation and repeated Unicode characters are treated like other characters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One character yields one; the empty input yields zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the substring by storing the best start and length.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Allow at most \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> distinct characters by tracking window frequencies.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count all distinct-character substrings by adding the current window length.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:567":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Determine whether some contiguous part of \u003ccode>text\u003c/code> is a rearrangement of all characters in \u003ccode>pattern\u003c/code>. Every pattern occurrence matters: for pattern \u003ccode>aab\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>baa\u003c/code> matches, whereas \u003ccode>abb\u003c/code> does not. Return a Boolean rather than the position.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original inputs consist of lowercase English letters, with a nonempty pattern. A matching part must have length exactly \u003ccode>len(pattern)\u003c/code>, and its 26 character frequencies must match the pattern's frequencies. If the pattern is longer than the text, no matching block exists.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Does any substring of s2 contain exactly the letters of s1, in any order?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any such substring has exactly len(s1) characters. How can you compare letter counts without recounting every candidate?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Fixed-size window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Slide a window of len(s1) over s2 with 26 count differences and a counter of letters whose difference is non-zero; each slide changes two letters, and a match is a zero counter.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(26) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every block of the required length, count its characters from scratch and compare the counts to the pattern counts. This takes O(nm) time for text length \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> and pattern length \u003ccode>m\u003c/code>, and O(26) count space. Sorting every block also works, but repeatedly sorts nearly identical data.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use a frequency difference: pattern count minus current window count. Initially subtract the first window. Maintain \u003ccode>nonzero\u003c/code>, the number of alphabet entries with a nonzero difference. Adjusting one letter requires checking whether its entry was zero before and after the adjustment. Exactly when \u003ccode>nonzero\u003c/code> is zero, all counts agree, so the current fixed-size block is a permutation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Each slide removes one old text character and inserts one new one. The adjustments restore the difference invariant even when the two characters are the same. Every possible starting position is visited once, so absence of a match after the scan proves the answer is false.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The signed entries may be positive or negative; neither sign alone determines a match. Their combined zero status captures both missing and excess occurrences. This avoids comparing all 26 counts after each shift, while still preserving multiplicities exactly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">pattern = ab; first text block = ei\u003c/p>\u003cpre>differences: a +1, b +1, e -1, i -1; nonzero = 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide through text eidba to block db\u003c/p>\u003cpre>differences: a +1, d -1; nonzero = 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Remove d and add a for block ba\u003c/p>\u003cpre>every difference is zero; return \u003cmark>True\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The order of letters does not matter; their signed frequency differences do.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def contains_permutation(pattern, text):\n    m = len(pattern)\n    if m &gt; len(text):\n        return False\n    diff = [0] * 26\n    for char in pattern:\n        diff[ord(char) - ord(\"a\")] += 1\n    nonzero = sum(value != 0 for value in diff)\n\n    def adjust(char, delta):\n        nonlocal nonzero\n        i = ord(char) - ord(\"a\")\n        nonzero -= diff[i] != 0\n        diff[i] += delta\n        nonzero += diff[i] != 0\n\n    for right, char in enumerate(text):\n        adjust(char, -1)\n        if right &gt;= m:\n            adjust(text[right - m], 1)\n        if right + 1 &gt;= m and nonzero == 0:\n            return True\n    return m == 0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Initialization takes O(m + 26), the scan O(n), and the difference array O(26) space. Each window move performs at most two constant-time adjustments.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A set loses multiplicities, so it cannot distinguish \u003ccode>aab\u003c/code> from \u003ccode>abb\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check only after a full-size window has formed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The code also treats an empty pattern as matching, even though the original domain excludes it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The character indexing assumes lowercase English letters.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Collect all matching start indices instead of stopping at the first one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a dictionary of differences for a much larger alphabet.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a streaming input, retain the last \u003ccode>m\u003c/code> characters in a queue.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:424":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find the longest contiguous string segment that can become all one character after replacing at most \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> positions. For \u003ccode>AABABBA\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>k = 1\u003c/code>, a segment of length 4 can be made uniform. We seek the length, not the actual replacements.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original alphabet is the 26 uppercase English letters and \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> is nonnegative. For any particular window, retaining the most frequent letter minimizes changes; its required replacement count is window length minus that maximum frequency. A window is valid exactly when this count is at most \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The longest substring that becomes a single repeated letter after at most k replacements.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For a given substring, how many replacements are needed? It depends on one count only.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Variable sliding window\u003c/p>\u003cp>A window needs length - (count of its most common letter) replacements; shrink while that exceeds k. The maximum count can even be kept as a running maximum that never decreases: a stale value never lets the window exceed a length that some valid window reached.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(26) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate every substring, count its letters, and test whether length minus the largest count is within the budget. Updating counts as each end advances gives O(26n²) time if the maximum is recomputed for each substring, and O(26) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain exact window counts and the largest frequency ever observed during this scan. This \u003ccode>peak\u003c/code> may become stale after removing a character, but it never decreases. If window length exceeds \u003ccode>peak + k\u003c/code>, remove from the left until it does not.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Why is a stale peak safe for the answer? When \u003ccode>peak\u003c/code> last increased to its current value, the then-current window really contained that many identical letters. The window length cannot decrease in this algorithm: adding one character increases it by one, and at most one removal is needed to restore the bound. So that earlier window was no longer than the current one. Extend the earlier window to the current length using already scanned characters: it retains those \u003ccode>peak\u003c/code> identical letters and needs at most \u003ccode>length - peak &lt;= k\u003c/code> replacements. Thus a valid window of the reported length exists even if the current window is invalid.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Conversely, an optimal valid window cannot be missed. To move the left edge past that window's start before its right end is reached, the algorithm would have to remove that start when the current window is a subarray of the optimum. That subarray is valid, and its true maximum frequency is at most the historical peak, so the removal condition could not hold. The maintained window therefore reaches at least the optimal length. Together these bounds prove the reported maximum is exact.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read AABA with k = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>peak A count = 3; length 4 needs one replacement; best = 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add B: length 5, peak 3, so 5 - 3 &gt; 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>remove first A; left becomes 1; historical peak remains 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Continue through BA\u003c/p>\u003cpre>lengths retained are at most 4; answer = \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The retained window need not itself always be valid under its current frequency counts; this version only promises the optimal length.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def longest_replacement(s, k):\n    counts = [0] * 26\n    left = 0\n    peak = 0\n    best = 0\n    for right, char in enumerate(s):\n        idx = ord(char) - ord(\"A\")\n        counts[idx] += 1\n        peak = max(peak, counts[idx])\n        while right - left + 1 - peak &gt; k:\n            counts[ord(s[left]) - ord(\"A\")] -= 1\n            left += 1\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each character enters and leaves at most once: O(n) time and O(26) auxiliary space. No scan of the alphabet is needed during shrinking.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A stale historical maximum is suitable for the length, not automatically for returning a valid witness.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With \u003ccode>k = 0\u003c/code>, only already uniform runs qualify.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replacing all occurrences of the least frequent letter is not always enough; sum all non-majority positions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The fixed array requires uppercase English letters.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Recompute the exact maximum from 26 counts to maintain a genuinely valid window and return its boundaries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a frequency dictionary for a different finite alphabet.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Assign different replacement costs; the simple majority formula no longer applies.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:713":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the nonempty contiguous subarrays whose product is strictly less than \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. For \u003ccode>[10, 5, 2, 6]\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>k = 100\u003c/code>, there are 8 such subarrays. A subarray is identified by its indices even if another subarray has the same values.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Entries are positive integers and \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> is an integer. Every nonempty product is at least one, so \u003ccode>k &lt;= 1\u003c/code> has answer zero. With positive integers, extending a window cannot decrease its product, and dropping factors cannot increase it. Zeros or fractional values require a different argument.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count contiguous subarrays whose product is below k, with positive values.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For a fixed right end, which left ends work? Are they scattered or one block?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> At most K, exactly K\u003c/p>\u003cp>Shrink while the product is at least k; then every start in [left, right] works, so add right - left + 1. Return 0 straight away when k &lt;= 1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each left endpoint, multiply successively through all right endpoints and increment a counter when the product is below \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. This inspects O(n²) subarrays and uses O(1) extra space. Breaking once the product reaches the threshold is possible under the stated positivity, but does not change the worst case.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each right endpoint, keep the longest suffix whose product is below \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. Multiply in the incoming value and remove left factors until the product becomes small enough. Because every factor is at least one, any suffix beginning later is also valid, and any earlier start was already too large. Thus there are exactly \u003ccode>right-left+1\u003c/code> valid subarrays ending here.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Adding that number for each right endpoint counts every valid subarray once, by its unique end. Integer division exactly removes a factor of the maintained product. The empty window has product one, which is below \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> after the early return.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An earlier start cannot become useful at a later end: its already excessive product is multiplied by more positive integer factors. Thus permanently discarding it is safe, and the algorithm does not have to reconsider old boundaries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">k = 100: add 10, then 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>products 10 and 50; add 1 then 2 to count\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add 2: product 100 is not strictly below 100\u003c/p>\u003cpre>remove 10; product 10; add 2 valid suffixes\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add 6: product 60\u003c/p>\u003cpre>add 3 suffixes; total = 1 + 2 + 2 + 3 = \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>When an individual factor is too large, it too is removed, leaving a zero-length contribution for that endpoint.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_product_less_than_k(nums, k):\n    if k &lt;= 1:\n        return 0\n    left = 0\n    product = 1\n    answer = 0\n    for right, value in enumerate(nums):\n        product *= value\n        while product &gt;= k:\n            product //= nums[left]\n            left += 1\n        answer += right - left + 1\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The pointers make O(n) total moves and use O(1) auxiliary variables. This is the usual arithmetic-operation bound; unbounded integer multiplication and division have costs depending on integer size.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Strict inequality requires shrinking at equality.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>k &lt;= 1\u003c/code> must be handled before the loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Ones do not increase the product but still generate many distinct subarrays.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not use this proof for zero or negative values.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count products at most \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> by changing the threshold comparison.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For positive real factors below one, the monotonicity assumptions need re-evaluation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return counts per right endpoint instead of their sum.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:904":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find the longest contiguous run containing at most two distinct item types. For \u003ccode>[1, 2, 1, 3, 3]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[2, 1, 3, 3]\u003c/code> has three types, but \u003ccode>[1, 3, 3]\u003c/code> has two, and the maximum length is 3. Identical values represent the same type.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Items arrive in a fixed order; only a consecutive run is allowed. Types may repeat arbitrarily. The input values can serve as dictionary keys. The frequency map needs to represent only the current window, including deleting a type once its count falls to zero.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The longest run that uses at most two different kinds of item.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Rephrase it as a window condition on the number of distinct values. What do you do when a third kind enters?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> At most K, exactly K\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a count per kind in the window and delete kinds whose count reaches zero; shrink from the left while there are more than two kinds, and record the length after each step.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space (at most three keys)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each start, extend a frequency map through all possible ends and update the best length whenever there are at most two types. This takes O(n²) expected time and up to O(n) space if the map is allowed to hold all types. Stopping at the third distinct type reduces unnecessary work but not the worst case.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Advance the right endpoint and increment the incoming type. While the map contains more than two keys, decrement the outgoing type and move left; remove a key when its count becomes zero. After this repair, the window is valid.\u003c/p>\u003cp>It is the longest valid suffix ending at the current right endpoint. Any start discarded during repair had at least three types when discarded; extending its end cannot remove a type. Those starts will therefore never become valid again. All later starts are suffixes of a valid window and are valid too. Comparing the window length at every end finds the optimum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read 1, 2, 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>counts {1: 2, 2: 1}; best = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read 3: three types\u003c/p>\u003cpre>remove 1 then 2; counts {1: 1, 3: 1}; left = 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read another 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window [1, 3, 3], length \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>; best stays 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Deleting zero counts is part of the invariant, not an optional cleanup: the dictionary's size is the number of types in the window.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def longest_two_types(items):\n    counts = {}\n    left = 0\n    best = 0\n    for right, value in enumerate(items):\n        counts[value] = counts.get(value, 0) + 1\n        while len(counts) &gt; 2:\n            old = items[left]\n            counts[old] -= 1\n            if counts[old] == 0:\n                del counts[old]\n            left += 1\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each item enters and leaves at most once, giving O(n) expected time with hash operations. At most three keys are present during repair, so auxiliary space is O(1). The empty sequence returns zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keeping zero-count keys falsely reports extra types.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The result is a contiguous run, not the total number of two globally frequent types.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One type throughout permits the whole input.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Shrink repeatedly: a single removal may not eliminate a type.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Permit at most \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> types and use O(k) map space during repair.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count valid runs by adding the window length for every right endpoint.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Store the best boundaries to return the run itself.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1658":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At each operation remove one element from either end of a positive integer array and subtract that value from \u003ccode>x\u003c/code>. Find the fewest removals that make the removed total exactly \u003ccode>x\u003c/code>, or return -1 if impossible. For \u003ccode>[1, 1, 4, 2, 3]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>x = 5\u003c/code>, removing the last two elements costs 2 operations.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original numbers and \u003ccode>x\u003c/code> are positive. Removed values consist of a prefix and a suffix, which do not overlap; the remaining values form one contiguous middle block, possibly empty. Since values are positive, a desired middle sum below zero is impossible.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Remove elements only from the two ends so that the removed ones sum to x, with as few removals as possible.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Look at what is left over rather than what is removed. What shape does it have?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Variable sliding window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Removing from both ends leaves one contiguous middle block, which must sum to total - x. Values are positive, so find the longest window with exactly that sum (shrink while the sum is too big); the answer is n minus its length, or -1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose the number of elements removed from the left and the right, with their sum at most \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>; evaluate their removed sum and minimize the number removed. Prefix and suffix running sums make each pair O(1), so there are O(n²) time and O(n) precomputation space. Testing all pairs without stored sums takes longer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let \u003ccode>target = sum(nums) - x\u003c/code>. Any successful removal leaves a middle block with sum \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>, and any such middle block can be obtained by removing the complementary prefix and suffix. Minimizing removals is therefore equivalent to maximizing the retained length.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use a window with nonnegative sum. Extend right, removing from the left while the sum exceeds \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>. Positivity ensures a discarded start cannot produce a target sum at a later end: that sum would only increase. After shrinking, a window equal to the target is the longest possible target-sum window ending there. When the target is zero, the empty window is a valid retained block, representing removal of all elements.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [1, 1, 4, 2, 3], x = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>total = 11; retained target = 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Extend through the 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window [1, 1, 4] sums to 6; longest retained length = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The remaining windows do not beat length 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>remove 5 - 3 = \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark> elements\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The complement is constructive: keep indices 0 through 2, remove indices 4 and 3 from the right.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def min_end_removals(nums, x):\n    target = sum(nums) - x\n    if target &lt; 0:\n        return -1\n    left = 0\n    total = 0\n    longest = 0 if target == 0 else -1\n    for right, value in enumerate(nums):\n        total += value\n        while left &lt;= right and total &gt; target:\n            total -= nums[left]\n            left += 1\n        if total == target:\n            longest = max(longest, right - left + 1)\n    return -1 if longest &lt; 0 else len(nums) - longest\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Computing the total and moving the two boundaries takes O(n) time. Only sums, boundaries and the best length are stored, so auxiliary space is O(1).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>If \u003ccode>x\u003c/code> equals the total, retaining an empty block gives \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>, not -1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If \u003ccode>x\u003c/code> exceeds the total, no removal sequence can work.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Checking only prefixes or only suffixes misses mixed removals.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Negative numbers invalidate this shrinking rule.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>With signed integers, use earliest prefix-sum indices to find a longest exact-sum subarray.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return counts removed from each side by retaining the best boundaries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Ask for the maximum number removed instead; then seek the shortest retained block.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:76":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a source string and a target string, find a shortest contiguous source substring containing every target character with at least its target multiplicity. For source \u003ccode>ADOBECODEBANC\u003c/code> and target \u003ccode>ABC\u003c/code>, one shortest answer is \u003ccode>BANC\u003c/code>. Return the empty string when no cover exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Characters are case-sensitive, and repeated target characters require repeated copies in the answer. The source and target need not have the same length or character set. The original task uses nonempty strings; this implementation also defines an empty target to have an empty answer. If several shortest covers exist, returning any one is acceptable; the strict update below keeps the earliest discovered one.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The length of the source is \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>, and the target has \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> distinct characters. Tracking only target characters avoids allowing irrelevant source characters to affect the validity counter. Hash operations are expected constant time, not an unconditional worst-case guarantee.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The shortest substring of s that contains every character of t, counting repeats.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Grow until the window covers t, then shrink while it still does. How can you tell \"covers\" in O(1)?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Minimum covering window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep need counts, window counts and formed, the number of distinct characters whose need is met; it changes only when a count crosses its threshold. Record while formed equals the number of distinct characters in t.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m + n) time, O(alphabet) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate every start and end, count the substring's characters, and check each target requirement. Maintaining counts as the end advances takes O(n²d) time if each candidate scans the distinct requirements, with O(d) relevant-count space. A naive recount of each substring can add another factor. A valid candidate need not have the same length as the target because irrelevant characters may lie between required ones.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store \u003ccode>need[c]\u003c/code> for each target character, \u003ccode>have[c]\u003c/code> for its count in the current window, and \u003ccode>formed\u003c/code> for the number of distinct target characters whose count meets or exceeds the requirement. Adding a character changes \u003ccode>formed\u003c/code> only when its count rises exactly to its threshold. Removing one changes \u003ccode>formed\u003c/code> only when its count was exactly at its threshold before removal. Thus \u003ccode>formed == len(need)\u003c/code> is equivalent to coverage, even with repeated characters and surplus copies.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Grow the right boundary one character at a time. While the window covers the target, record it if it is shorter than the best and move the left boundary rightward. Once a removal makes the window invalid, the next rightward addition may restore coverage. Every removed start has already produced its shortest covering window for the current right endpoint; extending that same start later would be longer, so it cannot lead to a better answer.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For completeness, consider any optimal window. When its right endpoint is processed, if its start has already been removed, a no-longer candidate starting there or earlier was recorded at an earlier endpoint. Otherwise the shrinking loop reaches its start or records a shorter covering window first. Thus the best recorded length is optimal. The invariant also explains why removing a surplus copy does not immediately invalidate coverage.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">source = ADOBECODEBANC, target = ABC\u003c/p>\u003cpre>after index 5: ADOBEC covers all three; best length 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Advance through index 10 and shrink the old start\u003c/p>\u003cpre>CODEBA covers ABC but has length 6; removing C makes it invalid\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At index 12, add C and shrink past O, D, E\u003c/p>\u003cpre>BANC covers; length \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>; removing B breaks coverage\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The check happens before each removal, so the last valid window is not lost. The stored start and length describe the actual source substring, not merely a count.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\n\ndef minimum_cover(source, target):\n    if not target:\n        return \"\"\n    need = Counter(target)\n    have = {char: 0 for char in need}\n    formed = 0\n    left = 0\n    best_start = 0\n    best_length = len(source) + 1\n    for right, char in enumerate(source):\n        if char in need:\n            have[char] += 1\n            if have[char] == need[char]:\n                formed += 1\n        while formed == len(need):\n            length = right - left + 1\n            if length &lt; best_length:\n                best_start, best_length = left, length\n            old = source[left]\n            if old in need:\n                if have[old] == need[old]:\n                    formed -= 1\n                have[old] -= 1\n            left += 1\n    if best_length &gt; len(source):\n        return \"\"\n    return source[best_start:best_start + best_length]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Building requirements takes O(m) time for target length \u003ccode>m\u003c/code>. Each source position enters once and leaves at most once, so the scan takes O(n) expected time. The maps use O(d) auxiliary space, excluding the O(answer length) returned slice. The returned empty string represents both no cover and the explicitly defined empty-target result.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Counting distinct characters alone misses multiplicity, for example target \u003ccode>AAB\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compare with the threshold, not merely a nonzero count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Removing an irrelevant character does not change \u003ccode>formed\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If a needed character never appears enough times, no best window is recorded.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A source shorter than the target cannot cover it, but needs no special case.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return boundary indices rather than materializing a substring.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find every minimum-length cover by collecting equal-length candidates.\u003c/li>\u003cli>On a stream, buffer enough source characters to return a witness; a length alone needs less retained text.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:992":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the nonempty contiguous subarrays containing exactly \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> distinct values. Distinctness concerns values, not positions: \u003ccode>[1, 2, 1]\u003c/code> has two distinct values even though it has three entries. For \u003ccode>[1, 2, 1, 2, 3]\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>k = 2\u003c/code>, the answer is 7.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original task uses positive integer values and a positive \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, but only hashability matters to the algorithm. We count index ranges separately, including equal-valued subarrays at different positions. A single longest valid window is not enough: for a given right endpoint, several different starts may yield exactly \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> types.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The count can be quadratic in \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>, although the algorithm performs only linear window operations. Python integers hold such counts without a fixed-width overflow. For an extension of the interface, \u003ccode>k &lt;= 0\u003c/code> returns zero because a nonempty subarray cannot contain exactly zero or fewer distinct values.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count subarrays with exactly K distinct integers.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Shrinking a window with exactly K distinct values can keep it at K, so one left pointer cannot capture all valid starts. Which related count is monotone?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> At most K, exactly K\u003c/p>\u003cp>Exactly K = atMost(K) - atMost(K - 1), where atMost adds right - left + 1 for each right end of a standard shrinking window over distinct counts.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every start, extend the end while recording frequencies, and add one whenever the number of keys equals \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. This inspects O(n²) windows, with O(n) worst-case auxiliary space for the frequency dictionary. Breaking when the number of types exceeds \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> can save work, but all-equal input remains quadratic.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>First solve the easier monotone problem: count subarrays with at most \u003ccode>limit\u003c/code> distinct values. Maintain a window with a frequency map and shrink from the left until it contains at most \u003ccode>limit\u003c/code> keys. For a fixed right endpoint, every suffix starting at or after \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> is valid; each earlier start is invalid because it was discarded only after a window had too many types, and extending the end cannot remove a type. Therefore add \u003ccode>right-left+1\u003c/code> valid subarrays at this endpoint.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The sets of subarrays with at most \u003ccode>k-1\u003c/code> and at most \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> types are nested. Their difference contains precisely those with exactly \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> types. Run the helper twice and subtract the totals. A zero limit is well defined: each insertion is removed immediately, yielding zero nonempty subarrays. A negative limit is handled as zero by the helper so the shrink loop cannot try to remove from an empty window.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The two passes may discard starts at different moments; this is intentional. Their difference counts individual index ranges, not a difference of arbitrary window lengths. Every nonempty range occurs in the at-most-k count exactly once at its right endpoint and is removed by the second count exactly when it has fewer than \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> types. This establishes both no omissions and no double counting.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [1, 2, 1, 2, 3], k = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>at-most-2 window lengths by right end: 1, 2, 3, 4, 2\ntheir sum is 12\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">For at most 1 type, each successive value changes type\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window lengths: 1, 1, 1, 1, 1; sum is 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Subtract nested counts\u003c/p>\u003cpre>exactly two types = 12 - 5 = \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>At the last endpoint of the first pass, the window \u003ccode>[2, 3]\u003c/code> is the longest permissible suffix. Its two possible starts contribute two at-most-two subarrays, but only one has exactly two types.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def exactly_k_distinct(nums, k):\n    def at_most(limit):\n        if limit &lt; 0:\n            return 0\n        counts = {}\n        left = 0\n        total = 0\n        for right, value in enumerate(nums):\n            counts[value] = counts.get(value, 0) + 1\n            while len(counts) &gt; limit:\n                old = nums[left]\n                counts[old] -= 1\n                if counts[old] == 0:\n                    del counts[old]\n                left += 1\n            total += right - left + 1\n        return total\n\n    if k &lt;= 0:\n        return 0\n    return at_most(k) - at_most(k - 1)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each pass moves each boundary at most \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> times, giving O(n) expected time across both passes with hash-table operations. The maps use O(min(n, k + 1)) auxiliary space for positive \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, including the temporarily inserted extra type. Empty input and a \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> larger than the number of types return zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Exactly-k windows are not simply all suffixes of the longest exactly-k window; shorter suffixes may have fewer types.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Delete zero-frequency entries before using the map size.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count only nonempty windows, especially when the limit is zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For \u003ccode>[1, 1, 1]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>k = 1\u003c/code>, the answer is 6, not 3.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Preserve the nested-count interpretation: subtract at most \u003ccode>k-1\u003c/code>, not at most \u003ccode>k+1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count at most \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> types directly with a single pass.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count exactly \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> odd entries using the same nested-set subtraction with an odd counter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Track two left boundaries in one pass to count the starts lying between them.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1838":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given an integer array and an operation budget \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, one operation increases one element by one. Return the largest frequency of any value achievable with at most \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> operations. For \u003ccode>[1, 2, 4]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>k = 5\u003c/code>, raising 1 and 2 to 4 costs 3 + 2, giving frequency 3.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original array is nonempty, its values are positive, and \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> is nonnegative. Values can be increased but never decreased. Their original order does not restrict which elements are chosen, so sorting does not change the answer. The implementation uses a sorted copy and does not mutate the input.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Up to k increments of +1; maximise how often one value appears.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Only raise values towards a value that already exists, and the nearest smaller values are the cheapest to raise. What order makes \"nearest\" contiguous?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Variable sliding window\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort. Window [l, r] can be levelled up to a[r] when a[r] * (r - l + 1) - window sum &lt;= k; this cost only grows as the window grows, so shrink while it exceeds k and keep the longest window.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each existing value, consider all subsets of values no larger than that target; calculate the total increase required for each subset and keep the largest affordable one. This costs O(n 2^n) time in a direct implementation and O(n) space for a subset. Enumerating all subsets emphasizes why selecting the cheapest values is useful.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An optimal target can be chosen as the largest original value among the selected elements: lowering a larger proposed target to that value never increases cost and does not reduce frequency. For a fixed target, the cheapest other elements to raise are the largest values not exceeding it. In sorted order they form a contiguous block ending at the target.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For sorted block \u003ccode>[left, right]\u003c/code>, the cost to raise everything to \u003ccode>a[right]\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>a[right] * length - sum(block)\u003c/code>. Extend right and shrink left until the cost is within budget. Adding a larger target cannot decrease the cost for an existing block, and removing its smallest value cannot increase it. Therefore any discarded start remains infeasible for later targets, and the repaired window is the largest affordable block ending here. Maximizing over endpoints finds the optimal frequency.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">sorted values [1, 2, 4], k = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window [1, 2]: cost 2 * 2 - 3 = 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window sum = 7; cost 4 * 3 - 7 = 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The full window fits\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best frequency = \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For a smaller budget, remove the smallest value until the same cost formula is within budget; the maximum is updated after this repair.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def max_frequency_after_increments(nums, k):\n    ordered = sorted(nums)\n    left = 0\n    total = 0\n    best = 0\n    for right, value in enumerate(ordered):\n        total += value\n        while value * (right - left + 1) - total &gt; k:\n            total -= ordered[left]\n            left += 1\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting takes O(n log n) time and O(n) space for the copy; the scan takes O(n) time and O(1) additional variables beyond that copy. An in-place sort can avoid the explicit copy but its sorting workspace is implementation-dependent.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The target is the rightmost value, not the mean; decreases are forbidden.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a wide enough integer type for \u003ccode>value * length\u003c/code> outside Python.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When \u003ccode>k = 0\u003c/code>, existing duplicates can still form the best answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>This function returns zero for an empty list as an extension.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the chosen target and original indices by sorting value-index pairs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If both increments and decrements are allowed, a median-based cost replaces the maximum-target cost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use prefix sums and binary search to find the largest feasible length.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:395":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find the length of the longest contiguous substring in which every character that appears appears at least \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> times. For \u003ccode>aaabb\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>k = 3\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>aaa\u003c/code> qualifies and the answer is 3. Characters absent from the chosen substring have no minimum requirement.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original input uses lowercase English letters, with positive \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. A substring can become invalid when a new character enters only once, and become valid again after more copies arrive. Consequently, simply shrinking whenever a character is underrepresented is not a sound monotone window rule. The alphabet contains at most 26 distinct letters.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The longest substring in which every character appears at least k times.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Adding a character can make the window better or worse, so no shrink rule is monotone. Which extra quantity, if you fixed it, would make one?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> At most K, exactly K\u003c/p>\u003cp>For each u = 1..26, run a window that allows at most u distinct letters, tracking how many letters reach k; whenever distinct == u and all u reach k, the window qualifies. Divide and conquer on letters rarer than k also works.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(26 n) time, O(26) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate each start and end and maintain character counts for the candidate. At each end inspect the at most 26 counts and record the length if every positive count is at least \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. This costs O(26n²) time and O(26) space. It is straightforward but repeatedly revisits overlapping substrings.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Fix a number \u003ccode>goal\u003c/code> of distinct characters and sweep a window with at most \u003ccode>goal\u003c/code> types. Track both the number of nonzero counts, \u003ccode>distinct\u003c/code>, and the number of types whose count has reached \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>qualified\u003c/code>. Shrink only when \u003ccode>distinct &gt; goal\u003c/code>, a monotone condition. When \u003ccode>distinct == goal == qualified\u003c/code>, the window itself meets the frequency condition.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Why do these passes find an optimum? Take any optimal substring with \u003ccode>u\u003c/code> types. During the pass for \u003ccode>goal = u\u003c/code>, at its right endpoint the maintained window begins no later than its left endpoint: that substring itself has at most \u003ccode>u\u003c/code> types, so shrinking cannot discard its start. The maintained window has exactly \u003ccode>u\u003c/code> types because it contains the optimum. Adding extra copies of those same types cannot make any of them fail the lower bound, so the maintained window is valid and at least as long as the optimum. Checking every \u003ccode>u\u003c/code> from 1 through 26 covers all possibilities.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">s = aaabb, k = 3, goal = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>after aaa: distinct = 1, qualified = 1; best = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add b: distinct = 2, so remove all three a characters\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window b has one type but qualified = 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pass goal = 2 sees aaabb\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a qualifies, b does not; best remains \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Counts cross the threshold in both directions. On removal, decrement \u003ccode>qualified\u003c/code> before lowering a count that currently equals \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def longest_at_least_k(s, k):\n    if k &lt;= 1:\n        return len(s)\n    best = 0\n    for goal in range(1, 27):\n        counts = [0] * 26\n        left = 0\n        distinct = 0\n        qualified = 0\n        for right, char in enumerate(s):\n            idx = ord(char) - ord(\"a\")\n            if counts[idx] == 0:\n                distinct += 1\n            counts[idx] += 1\n            if counts[idx] == k:\n                qualified += 1\n            while distinct &gt; goal:\n                old = ord(s[left]) - ord(\"a\")\n                if counts[old] == k:\n                    qualified -= 1\n                counts[old] -= 1\n                if counts[old] == 0:\n                    distinct -= 1\n                left += 1\n            if distinct == goal and qualified == goal:\n                best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 26 passes, each O(n), for O(26n) time and O(26) auxiliary space. For a variable alphabet of size \u003ccode>A\u003c/code>, this becomes O(An) time and O(A) space if a suitable indexed alphabet is provided.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A letter at frequency one is not automatically a reason to discard it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Only characters present in the substring must reach \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>k = 1\u003c/code> makes the entire string valid; \u003ccode>k &gt; len(s)\u003c/code> yields zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>This array implementation assumes lowercase English characters.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Recursively split at characters whose total frequency in a segment is below \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return a witness by recording the best boundaries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a general alphabet, enumerate its distinct characters and use a frequency dictionary.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
